// @check
// @error A Fd-keyed Map isn't supported yet
// THE GATE'S REMAINING JOB, pinned beside `newtype-key-annotation.vl`. Resolving a map
// key through its LOWERING rather than its spelling admits a newtype over `i32`/`string`
// — it must not admit a newtype over a base the map rep has no hash for.
//
// A BOUNDARY PIN, not behavioural coverage of the resolution rule: the spelling was
// refused before `mapKeyRepTy` existed too, so reverting that helper leaves this file
// green. What it catches is the OTHER direction — a later widening of the gate (erasing a
// brand without checking what it erases TO) flips it.
//
// `type Fd = new f64` erases to `f64`, which has no hash (the rep has exactly two,
// `__str_hash__` and the integer mix), so the brand changes nothing: it is refused with
// the same wording its base is, naming the SPELLING the user wrote.
//
// The literal-union key this file used to pin as a second negative is now ADMITTED —
// `litunion-map-key-every-consumer.vl` is its positive — because the key render and
// `.keys()`'s element type moved together, which is what that pin asked for (D1748).
type Fd = new f64

const fm: {[Fd]: i32} = Map()
